in @prism
in @glass
in @meta
in @mirror/store

# @mirror/au — the Fate-emitted form; the lifecycle position before
# settle through the property chain.
#
# Recognition (docs/specs/au-and-conductivity.md): au IS gold at the
# artifact altitude. Same operation (settle) applied at different
# altitudes produces different au:
#   au(@code/rust)   is a binary
#   au(@release)     is a signed archive
#   au(@ci/github)   is an action YAML
# The altitude parametrizes the emit; au is the type of the settled
# result. Per the spec's thesis: au IS the output type of @fate
# inference. The five Fate models do not return strings, ASTs,
# decisions, or text; they return values of type au. Verification is
# a predicate over au (conductivity), not a type-level transform
# producing a different kind.
#
# Relationship to `fragment` (per docs/specs/mosaic-as-type-system.md
# §2.3, upserted 2026-06-06):
#
# au and fragment have byte-identical record shapes. The distinction
# is **lifecycle position, not type altitude**:
#
#   au       — what Fate emits. Content-addressed, declared altitude,
#              transparency carries whatever inference left unverified.
#              Uncommitted in the sense that the property chain has
#              not yet been run.
#   fragment — what @mirror/store keeps. Committed in the sense that
#              settle has run au through the property chain and the
#              verdict landed.
#   settle   — the act of running au through the compiler and the
#              property chain. Settling IS verification. As a Kleisli
#              arrow in the Imperfect monad (shards/glass.mirror's
#              imperfect(a, e, l)):
#                settle: au → Imperfect<fragment>
#              with Pass → fragment committed; Partial → fragment
#              committed with located transparency; Failure → au
#              stays in Fate's buffer for the next kintsugi tick.
#
# Type-level discipline distinguishing them lives at SpectralUuid
# validity in the target OID graph, enforced by the property chain at
# commit time. The substrate-pull-realize move — lift `content: oid`
# to `content: SpectralUuid` on `fragment` once SpectralUuid is
# declared at the shard altitude — is captured as deferred work in
# docs/specs/mosaic-as-type-system.md §9.7.
#
# Reconciliation with mosaic.mirror (C2): mosaic's algebraic claim
#   type au(altitude) = settle(altitude)
# is what au IS at the build altitude — the settle operation's
# return type, parametric over altitude. This shard provides the
# concrete record form (content + altitude + transparency) that
# realizes the algebra against the @mirror/store. They compose:
# mosaic.au IS this au; the algebraic equation in mosaic.mirror is
# the substrate-pull statement; the record form below is the
# substrate-pull realization.
#
# Spec context: docs/specs/au-and-conductivity.md (the type Fate
# produces; gold is the metal that conducts; au is the type that
# carries), docs/specs/mosaic.md (settle emitter -> au), the
# transparency floor in shards/glass.mirror.
# Substrate decisions: [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]],
#                      [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]],
#                      [[architecture-au-conductivity]] (au is the
#                      Fate output type; gold conducts; verification
#                      IS conductivity in context).
# Related shards: glass.mirror (transparency / imperfect carrier),
#                 store.mirror (oid — au's content-address carrier),
#                 mosaic.mirror (the build altitude that emits au).
# Path-namespace property: this file at `shards/mirror/au.mirror`
# declares `@mirror/au` and only that.

prism @mirror/au {
  focus au
  project au
  split au
  shift au
  settle au
}

# === au at an altitude — the concrete record form ===
#
# au is parametric over altitude. au(@code/rust), au(@release), and
# au(@ci/github) are distinct types (the altitude is part of the
# type, not a runtime tag). The record below is what each au(A)
# resolves to:
#
#   content       — the settled artifact, content-addressed in the
#                   @mirror/store. The bytes live behind the oid;
#                   au carries the reference, not the payload.
#   altitude      — which altitude this au settled at. `ref` is the
#                   substrate's altitude-reference carrier; the
#                   altitude is what the type-parameter `A` binds.
#   transparency  — residual opacities, if any. Per glass.mirror's
#                   transparency<p>, success means the settle was
#                   clean; partial/failure carries the opacity map
#                   kintsugi reads to pick the next focus.
#
# Identity contract: au's identity IS the content-hash of
# (altitude, content, transparency). Per the substrate's content-
# addressing (shards/store.mirror, shards/prism.mirror's spectral-
# triple framing under CoincidenceHash<5,5>), an au value settles
# to a single oid under hashing of its three fields. Two au values
# with the same (altitude, content, transparency) are the same
# au — byte-equal at the oid. This is the substrate-level statement
# of relational entanglement: change the altitude or the residual
# transparency and you get a different au, even if `content` is the
# same bytes.
type au(altitude) = {
  content: oid,
  altitude: ref,
  transparency: transparency(altitude),
}

# === surface ===
#
# identity(a) -> oid
#   au's content-address. The substrate computes the oid by hashing
#   (altitude, content, transparency); two au values with equal
#   identity ARE the same au. The contract above made operational.
identity(a: au) -> oid { \ }

# verify(a) -> verdict
#   The conductivity check at au's home altitude. Per au-and-
#   conductivity.md: verification IS conductivity in context;
#   `verify` reduces au's transparency over the altitude's property
#   set to a single verdict. `pass` means the residual opacity
#   weighs zero (the settle was clean); `partial(c)` means the
#   settle is conducting at confidence c; `failure(r)` means the
#   au does not carry signal at this altitude.
verify(a: au) -> verdict { \ }

# shatter(a) -> ref
#   Project au onto the .shatter disk form (per mosaic.md §7 and
#   the forward reference to shards/mirror/shatter.mirror). The
#   substrate writes a five-section file holding the AST, the
#   transparency, the properties, the kernel, and the fate of the
#   settled artifact. .shatter IS the disk projection of au +
#   splinter + mosaic; this action is the projection itself.
shatter(a: au) -> ref { \ }

out @mirror/au
out au
out identity
out verify
out shatter
